---
SCORING DIMENSIONS

Score each applicable dimension on a 1–5 scale (1 = very poor, 5 = excellent).

operation_appropriateness — Did the system execute the correct clustering operations in response to each oracle message? A score of 5 means the system correctly interpreted and executed the intended operation type each turn; a score of 1 means operations were wrong, ignored, or repeatedly incorrect.

label_accuracy — Do the cluster labels and summaries accurately describe the intended cluster result? A score of 5 means labels are precise and reflect the desired characteristics; a score of 1 means labels are misleading, generic, or contradicting.

clustering_coherence — Do the films within each cluster genuinely belong together? For each cluster, consider whether the exemplar films share a common characteristic consistent with the cluster's label, and whether the grouping is tight or overly broad. A score of 5 means every cluster has internally consistent, clearly distinguishable members; a score of 1 means clusters are incoherent, mixing unrelated films, or so broad as to be meaningless.
{% if has_suggestion %}

suggestion_meaningfulness — Were the system's proactive suggestions (when offered) relevant and actionable given the current clustering state? A score of 5 means every suggestion was insightful and appropriate; a score of 1 means suggestions were irrelevant or repetitive.
{% endif %}
{% if has_explanation %}

explanation_quality — The system explained its reasoning during this session (see transcript). Were those explanations clear, accurate, and helpful? A score of 5 means explanations were precise and added real value; a score of 1 means they were vague, incorrect, or unhelpful.
{% endif %}

intent_alignment — Does the final clustering state reflect the oracle's overall intent description, considering the full trajectory? A score of 5 means the final clusters closely realise the intended exploration; a score of 1 means the final state ignores or contradicts the intent.
{% if axis_contexts %}

concept_axis_quality — For each concept axis the system built (listed above), rate the quality of its construction. Consider: (a) Bipolar coherence — do the two poles form a genuine, sensible opposing spectrum? (b) Discriminativeness — would these poles meaningfully separate films? (c) Naming — are the axis name and pole labels concise, accurate, and non-generic? (d) Space appropriateness — was semantic (text) or visual (trailer) embedding the right choice? Give one overall score across all axes. A score of 5 means every axis has coherent, discriminative, well-named poles in the right space; a score of 1 means poles are not genuine opposites, are generic/mislabeled, or the wrong space was used.
{% endif %}

---
OUTPUT FORMAT

Respond with valid JSON only. The JSON must contain a "scores" array with exactly one element per dimension listed above, in the order listed. Each element must have:
- "dimension": the dimension name string
- "score": an integer from 1 to 5
- "rationale": one sentence explaining the score

Example (showing the always-present dimensions; include optional ones only when listed above):
{
  "scores": [
    {"dimension": "operation_appropriateness", "score": 4, "rationale": "Operations were correct on most turns; one merge was interpreted as a focus."},
    {"dimension": "label_accuracy", "score": 5, "rationale": "Labels precisely matched the exemplar films shown."},
    {"dimension": "clustering_coherence", "score": 4, "rationale": "Most clusters were internally consistent; one group mixed unrelated films."},
    {"dimension": "intent_alignment", "score": 4, "rationale": "Final clusters closely matched the oracle's stated intent."}
  ]
}
